WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.604
Film Courage: What are the&nbsp;
best ways to build plot twists?

00:00:02.604 --> 00:00:06.360
Andrew Warren, Author/Screenwriter: I think&nbsp;
you have to find what's organic to your story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.920
I do think you need to surprise the reader.&nbsp;
I do try to sometimes when I think of a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.380
I'll try to think “Okay this is the way&nbsp;
the scene would go but what if we went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.240
this way instead? I haven't seen that.” But&nbsp;
I also think you need to be really honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.900
with yourself when you're doing that,&nbsp;
does this work better because because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.840
you haven't seen it doesn't mean it works&nbsp;
and I do I would rather be effective than

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.520
I mean sure it's good I do try to create&nbsp;
new things I do try to go in unexpected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.220
directions but if you're going in a direction&nbsp;
that's not satisfying I think you're sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.920
you're writing for yourself and not for your&nbsp;
readers at that point I mean as an example I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:01:00.060
wrote a short story once about a a guy who is&nbsp;
a werewolf and every time the full moon comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.700
out he chains himself up in the basement and he&nbsp;
tranquilizes himself so they won't hurt anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.160
and then he has this whole process of how he&nbsp;
gets out of it the next day and then one day he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.200
does all this but someone is still mauled in the&nbsp;
town he's in and so it's sort of trying to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.040
out well how could this have happened I know I&nbsp;
was chained up I know I did everything and he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.120
trying to figure out what's going on and when you&nbsp;
get to the end of the story you find out there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.920
another werewolf that came to town and to me I&nbsp;
thought that was really cool but I remember one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.900
of my friends read it and I was oh I was kind of&nbsp;
disappointed because I really was I wanted to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.540
how he figured out how to get out I thought that&nbsp;
was going to be the whole thing that maybe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.400
wolf side of him is becoming smarter something&nbsp;
that along those lines and I had never even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.360
thought about that but I it made me think that for&nbsp;
him he found that story unsatisfying and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.700
it's better to satisfy the reader when you can&nbsp;
than be different for the sake of being different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.700
you said you'd rather be effective than be novel&nbsp;
yeah I mean I'd rather the scene works and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.380
the reader enjoys it and feels something from&nbsp;
it than to be different for the sake of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:14.160
different I mean you can it's and sometimes it&nbsp;
can be easy to be different in a very cheap way I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.800
mean if you if you're writing a a spy thrower and&nbsp;
you get to the end and the hero fails sure that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:26.220
different but it's probably not very satisfying&nbsp;
to the reader unless you make that failure mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:32.040
something or matter or lead to something else&nbsp;
that's interesting but if it's oh you thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.700
this would happen and instead this happens but&nbsp;
there's it's disconnected from everything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.800
or it's not satisfying to the reader I think&nbsp;
that that's being novel but you're not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.960
effective you're not you're not satisfying the&nbsp;
reader you're not presenting a a compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:53.760
story end every chapter on a cliffhanger or&nbsp;
surprise ending I if you can without it being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.680
forced I mean I always try to I think your goal&nbsp;
at the end of the chapter is to make the reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.900
read the next chapter right so Cliffhanger is&nbsp;
a really good way to do that but it's not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:09.120
only way I mean I think even if you've sort of&nbsp;
resolved the drama of that scene you can sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:17.040
give a hint that yeah he got through this but&nbsp;
there's more coming or things aren't as safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.620
as they seem you I think you always try to leave&nbsp;
with a little bit of I don't know what to call it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.980
not really a cliffhanger per se but a a sense of&nbsp;
danger kind of what you were saying before that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.860
kind of mood that yeah he survived this but he's&nbsp;
certainly not safe there's there's the story is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:42.000
still moving would you put a surprise revelation&nbsp;
to something that keeps people a little bit on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.500
their edge of their seat but curious I think&nbsp;
actually one of my editors said what I tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.600
to do and they they this oh I the way you end&nbsp;
chapters I usually try to find something sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.800
symbolic in the language that might be a little&nbsp;
sinister if they've gotten through a big red car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:05.820
chase and it's sunset I might say the sun dripped&nbsp;
red blood across the sky as they vanished into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.800
horizon or something that where it's it's not&nbsp;
really literally implying anything but it gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.800
you the sort of mood of danger and I remember an&nbsp;
editor had said oh I really the way you end your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:29.520
visual cues of of something sinister and how do&nbsp;
you start the chapter varies yeah I mean I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:36.780
I think you want to try to get in late and get out&nbsp;
right at the right time I mean try not to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:43.740
too much time with Exposition I mean you need a&nbsp;
little but I try to do the bare minimum and I try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:51.660
to start I mean it sounds kind of silly to say&nbsp;
but I try to start where it's most interesting I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.520
mean if two people are in an argument I don't&nbsp;
necessarily need to see what led up to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.080
argument I want to start with the argument if&nbsp;
they're arguing about whether they should send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.180
someone into this territory to get an agent out&nbsp;
I don't need to see the girl walk up go in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.140
elevator walk into the office and sit down for the&nbsp;
briefing and then get into the argument I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:15.360
start with the argument should we go in and help&nbsp;
this guy or not that's the point of the scene so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.260
I think that kind of comes down to knowing&nbsp;
what the point of the chapter is I mean I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:25.200
think that every chapter should have a reason for&nbsp;
being there and when I say chapter it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.760
interchangeable to me with scene I tend to write&nbsp;
one scene per chapter but not everybody does so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.880
each scene needs to have a reason if the reason is&nbsp;
exposition and sometimes that's necessary I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.620
you need to make it as short as possible as quick&nbsp;
as possible give them what they need to know and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.200
move on is that something you've perfected over&nbsp;
the years because in the beginning we want to sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:51.300
down and pour all of this out I don't know if I'd&nbsp;
say I've perfected it I mean I think I was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:57.060
aware of it because I said I'd written screenplays&nbsp;
and things beforehand and I do think screenplays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.860
kind of teach you to be very economical with your&nbsp;
language because you're so constrained by time

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:09.720
so I don't think I necessarily had a huge&nbsp;
problem with it but I do I will say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:17.280
writing that first book I definitely would be&nbsp;
more inclined to add things because I'm oh man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.300
I don't know if I can make it to 80 000 words or&nbsp;
a hundred thousand words whatever in my head it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.520
was going to be so I'm all right I'll talk about&nbsp;
this and I would sort of go off on tangents I I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:32.340
think through editing most of the excess there&nbsp;
was trimmed back but I don't even then it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.760
massive I mean I think the difference between the&nbsp;
finished edited book and the original manuscript&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:44.820
was probably maybe 10 or 12 percent so it wasn't&nbsp;
a huge amount but I do remember that sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.680
I don't know if I can write this many words I&nbsp;
better talk about this and talk about this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.540
and then and then when you do it you realize oh&nbsp;
no it's I actually don't have room for all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:07:01.140
I got to get to the point because and move on&nbsp;
how important is setting up Clues I think it I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:05.220
mean it certainly depends on what you're writing&nbsp;
if you're writing a mystery it's super important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:13.740
my stories I wouldn't say they're really&nbsp;
Mysteries per se they tend to be more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.260
kind of this journey that King goes on&nbsp;
in each one so it's I don't think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:27.360
he's not necessarily trying to find out who&nbsp;
killed suspect a or something that it's more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.500
you need to go find this guy and kill&nbsp;
him or you need to go find this guy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:37.020
get this laptop that he has and I tend to&nbsp;
somehow connect the goal to something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.740
happened in his past so as he goes on&nbsp;
this Mission he's sort of reflecting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:47.280
something in his past that troubles him or that&nbsp;
that he failed or that is a source of guilt for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.620
him so they're sort of if not directly linked then&nbsp;
kind of metaphorically linked if you're writing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.720
mystery obviously the clues are really important&nbsp;
but I think in any case whether you're writing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:03.120
mystery or even with me with where I've sometimes&nbsp;
I have a scenario where there's a double agent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:09.600
and someone is revealed to not be who you thought&nbsp;
they were and usually that's planned out but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.620
have been instances where I've been writing a&nbsp;
scene I thought this would be so much better if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:19.500
this person was the traitor and the great thing&nbsp;
about writing is you can always go back and add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.780
in seed in those clues so if that's why I said&nbsp;
before it's good to divert from your outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.580
sometimes and surprise yourself there's nothing&nbsp;
stopping you from going back where you introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.260
that character and maybe add in something that&nbsp;
makes them seem a little shifty or add in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:41.160
motive that you didn't have before I think people&nbsp;
tend to think of writing linearly and for the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.000
part part you do but there's nothing stopping&nbsp;
you from jumping around and changing things.

